  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transforming Kenya`s local food systems through climate-friendly home-grown school feeding</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya is aiming to reach 10 million children by 2030 through the scale-up of the school meals programme, a key engine of sustainable food systems transformation. This safety net is critical for children in arid and semi-arid land counties (ASALs), regularly hit by climate shocks. Leveraging on the expertise of the Rome Based Agencies (RBAs: FAO, IFAD, WFP), the JP will support the scale-up of the national climate-friendly home-grown school meals programme, in line with the National School Meals Coalition (NSMC) and Kenya&apos;s pathways for food systems transformation. 

The intervention will critically support government to source quality food locally for the school meals programme (instead of relying on imports) including through the adoption of the aggregator model, while at the same time promoting adoption of healthy and diversified diets in schools; and support the government to set-up sustainable financing models to sustain the scale-up of the school meals programme.

Building on the lessons from the SDG Fund seed funding in Embu and Tharaka Nithi in 2024/ 2025, the JP will seek to test the aggregator model in other two counties in Kenya, namely Baringo and West Pokot. In line with Government?s scale-up plan, the work in these counties will complement other initiatives implemented by the Rome Based Agencies (RBAs). 

